# Cloudflare Pages rewrites for the anonymous link surface.
#
# `expo export -p web` emits dynamic routes as files named after the parameter,
# so a real token URL is a 404 until the host rewrites to one. 200 = rewrite
# (serve that file, keep the URL), not a redirect — the client router then reads
# the real token off location.pathname.
#
# The targets are NOT the exported filenames. Those contain square brackets,
# which Cloudflare Pages cannot serve: it redirects `/s/[token]` to itself and a
# reader gets ERR_TOO_MANY_REDIRECTS. Measured on a live deployment, 2026-08-27
# — the risk spike S-2 F-3 left open. `scripts/prepare-link-surface.js` renames
# them to the bracket-free names below, and `npm run export:web` runs it.
#
# No `.html` either: Pages serves `foo.html` at `/foo` and 308s the `.html`
# form, so a target ending in `.html` bounces before it can be rewritten.
#
# ORDER MATTERS. Pages takes the first matching rule, so deeper routes come
# first: with only the catch-all at the bottom, `/s/<token>/references` and
# `/s/<token>/crew/<id>` would both be served the shoot view. That is a silently
# wrong page, not an error, which is why the order is asserted in the tests
# rather than trusted.
#
# Every route added under `/s/` needs a rule here AND an entry in
# prepare-link-surface.js. A route with no rule is a 404 for every real URL.
/s/:token/crew/:crewId /s/link-crew 200
/s/:token/references /s/link-references 200
/s/:token /s/link 200
